# thirdpart/musl-libc-algorithms.manifest — vendored musl algorithms
#
# Provenance (libc-completion plan T11, "固定 musl 1.2.5 源档来源和 SHA256"):
#   Archive : https://musl.libc.org/releases/musl-1.2.5.tar.gz
#   SHA256  : a9a118bbe84d8764da0ea0d28b3ab3fae8477fc7e4085d90102b8596fc7c75e4
#   License : standard MIT (see musl-libc-algorithms/COPYRIGHT, copied
#             verbatim from the archive's COPYRIGHT file).
#
# What is vendored, where, and how it was adapted
# -----------------------------------------------
# libc/stdlib/floatscan.c
#   from src/internal/floatscan.c (507 lines)
#   adaptation diff (this is the full list; everything else is verbatim):
#     - #include <stdio.h>/<math.h> replaced by the private
#       <stdlib/floatscan.h> + libc's own <errno.h>/<ctype.h>/<float.h>;
#       `hidden` dropped (the scanner is static to this archive's TU).
#     - FILE* replaced by the memory-only shfile_t pseudo-FILE
#       (libc/include/stdlib/floatscan.h): musl's shgetc/shunget/shcnt/
#       shlim/sh_fromstring macros with __uflow and the real FILE
#       implementation NOT imported — strtod-family input is always a
#       NUL-terminated memory string.  Behaviour difference, deliberate:
#       shgetc answers EOF sticky at the terminating NUL without
#       advancing (musl reads the NUL byte and advances), which keeps
#       musl's shunget bookkeeping balanced and the POSIX endptr exact.
#     - the four math calls are vendored in the same file as static
#       helpers (see below), because OS01's freestanding libc has no
#       libm to answer scalbn/scalbnl/copysignl/fmodl.  The shipped
#       usr/lib/libm.a (2026-11-21) carries the double and float base
#       algebra only, so the long double variants still have to be
#       answered locally.
#     - errno=EINVAL on no-conversion is SUPPRESSED by the wrappers in
#       libc/stdlib/strtod.c (they save/restore errno around a
#       zero-consumption scan): musl sets EINVAL there, glibc/POSIX
#       practice and our T10 strtox discipline preserve errno.  The
#       scanner itself is untouched.
#     - no-conversion detection moved to the WRAPPERS: the scanner's
#       EINVAL flags it, the wrappers answer endptr = nptr and restore
#       errno (musl leaves endptr past the whitespace and keeps
#       EINVAL; glibc answers nptr + untouched errno, and the oracle
#       table pins that).
#     - subnormal ERANGE raised to glibc's practice: EVERY inexact
#       subnormal result answers ERANGE (musl only some).  EXACT
#       subnormals stay silent — "0x1p-1074" answers errno=0 like
#       glibc — so the check applies to decimal inputs only.
#     - "nan(<n-chars>)" payload support added (mk_nan): the payload
#       digits are read as a DECIMAL number into the low mantissa
#       bits with the sign bit honoured ("nan(123)" / "-nan" match
#       glibc bit-for-bit).  musl returns a payload-less positive NaN
#       here; __builtin_nanf(payload) was rejected because a runtime
#       string turns the builtin into a libm nanf() call and OS01's
#       libm.a does not provide nanf.  Non-digit n-chars end the
#       payload (simplification; the test table only exercises digit
#       payloads).
#     - the strtod/strtof wrappers narrow NaNs BIT-EXACTLY (sign +
#       payload rebuilt from the 80-bit mantissa): the x87 narrowing
#       instruction would otherwise drop the payload of "nan(123)"
#       when producing a float/double.
#
#   static helpers inside libc/stdlib/floatscan.c (same MIT license):
#     - copysignl  from src/math/copysignl.c   (LDBL_MANT_DIG==64 branch)
#     - scalbn     from src/math/scalbn.c
#     - scalbnl    from src/math/scalbnl.c     (LDBL_MANT_DIG==64 branch)
#     - fmodl      from src/math/fmodl.c       (LDBL_MANT_DIG==64 branch)
#     with libm.h's union ldshape (little-endian 80-bit x87 layout)
#     trimmed in, isnan(x) written as x != x, fabsl/copysign delegated
#     to compiler builtins (no libcall on x86_64).
#
# The OS01 libc/include/libc_arch/<arch>/float_abi.h fs_fabs helper is
# NOT vendored from musl: it is an OS01 file, and it performs the sign
# bit clear with integer bit operations on the arch's scanner working
# type (x86_64 long double, aarch64 double).  It used to call
# __builtin_fabs/__builtin_fabsl; the integer form was adopted on
# 2026-11-21 so the scanner core never asks the FPU for arithmetic and
# the sign of a zero and the payload of a NaN survive unchanged.
#
# Patches to the module after vendoring
# -------------------------------------
# None yet.  If musl fixes a floatscan bug after 1.2.5, the patch is
# reviewed against the adaptation diff above (only the listed deltas
# are OS01-specific; everything else applies cleanly) and this manifest
# gains a dated entry describing what was taken and why.
#
# Not applicable / deliberately not imported
# ------------------------------------------
# src/internal/stdio_impl.h, __uflow, the real FILE machinery — replaced
# by the memory pseudo-FILE (see above).
# src/internal/shgetc.c/shgetc.h — folded into the pseudo-FILE macros in
# libc/include/stdlib/floatscan.h (the __shlim/__shgetc functions only
# serve real-FILE and width-limited scanf, neither of which this port
# has).
# wcsto*-family callers (need real FILE pushback semantics) — OS01 has
# no wchar layer.
# musl's locale-dependent scanning — musl floatscan has none.

# The base-algebra math set (libm) — 2026-11-21
# ---------------------------------------------
# Provenance: the same archive and SHA256 as the floatscan entry above.
#   Archive : https://musl.libc.org/releases/musl-1.2.5.tar.gz
#   SHA256  : a9a118bbe84d8764da0ea0d28b3ab3fae8477fc7e4085d90102b8596fc7c75e4
#   License : standard MIT (see musl-libc-algorithms/COPYRIGHT).
#
# Vendored into libc/math/ and built into the standalone usr/lib/libm.a
# (NOT part of libc.a, NOT part of libk.a — the kernel stays FP-free).
#
# Upstream file -> vendored file, with the upstream SHA256:
#   src/math/fabs.c      -> libc/math/fabs.c
#     99880a7802d67077fa54a2bbb5d3388cf42b00c96d3e2b1c16d63f2918157754
#   src/math/fabsf.c     -> libc/math/fabsf.c
#     8fea1977b6b8913e7db76d8576f95f30fceb35e47122af299fb49dd452cf74c8
#   src/math/copysign.c  -> libc/math/copysign.c
#     7a5ae1fbedc6da808c69b1769f21624c20709c93eaa846fdf89546ab6bccecae
#   src/math/copysignf.c -> libc/math/copysignf.c
#     71d74b1169dbea28d12adbd98a30975777b79d951f03b1111480911922fe37d0
#   src/math/trunc.c     -> libc/math/trunc.c
#     2f2941542db65a5c3a8a6fa952b978a5ff757f8b14ffdaef8ad28e0cafead644
#   src/math/truncf.c    -> libc/math/truncf.c
#     dd67c4d71894da6c93bc41ec6a141de45124d3d3f4d012bc95aac4fc792abccb
#   src/math/floorf.c    -> libc/math/floorf.c
#     55a62f12a4bcd151ca3a82e9ec98b1744c15017f1b6cdea56b33220ffdb166f3
#   src/math/ceilf.c     -> libc/math/ceilf.c
#     691d3a8e183ebe5f09a68120766877872e10f9267fe6e2bba0341b93d2863cc8
#   src/math/fmod.c      -> libc/math/fmod.c
#     33fb61093e04783081b9e8f8a8a303456b2f919c215cd0c378c1ed0acf41934c
#   src/math/fmodf.c     -> libc/math/fmodf.c
#     eecc1cf499f143181880a33a95f1614be323aca704815a7b57d4969a7cacc2d4
#   src/math/sqrt.c      -> libc/math/sqrt.c
#     688908f94c3edf7a439cb64094034a596a1fbff08445b9158a22874cb52ead4f
#   src/math/sqrtf.c     -> libc/math/sqrtf.c
#     bd7c19c2c4efdbd1259f04a919c88f7119d9402cdd41584c7b0f854acb203b42
#   src/math/sqrt_data.c -> libc/math/sqrt_data.c
#     590aaadff964dcfc36ef9e5302423a779cda4f1608e15dbeee8e43e6e5f1c6fe
#   src/math/sqrt_data.h -> libc/math/sqrt_data.h
#     5fa47a8b8c1afbee4dcd10f71d2f6c433a24f9aa5ce7a90924cd1db236cbc03c
#
# New OS01 sources (no upstream counterpart):
#   libc/math/musl_libm.h — the private helper header (asuint64/asdouble/
#     asuint/asfloat, predict_true/predict_false, the fixed quiet NaN
#     patterns).  It replaces musl's src/internal/libm.h, which carries
#     musl-internal machinery (long double unions, fp_arch.h,
#     WANT_ROUNDING) that OS01 does not have.
#   libc/math/floor.c, ceil.c — integer ports of musl's float variants
#     (the double variants use the 1/EPS toint trick, whose correctness
#     depends on excess-precision semantics).
#   libc/math/round.c, roundf.c — integer ports of musl's round.
#
# Adaptation diff (13 items, as specified in the design spec):
#   1. #include "libm.h" -> #include "musl_libm.h" (private header).
#   2. isnan(y) -> an integer bit test (exponent all ones, fraction
#      non-zero).  "y != y" compiles to a compare instruction.
#   3. __math_invalid(x) -> the fixed quiet NaN 0x7ff8000000000000
#      (float: 0x7fc00000).
#   4. the error paths are split per the errno contract: a NaN operand is
#      not an error (errno untouched), an infinite dividend and a zero
#      divisor are domain errors (EDOM).
#   5. FENV_SUPPORT -> 0 (OS01 has no fenv).
#   6. the FORCE_EVAL(x + 0x1p120f) inexact triggers are removed.
#   7. floor/ceil (double) and round/roundf are ported to integer bit
#      manipulation.
#   8. fmod/fmodf: (x*y)/(x*y) -> the fixed quiet NaN.
#   9. fmod/fmodf: 0*x -> an integer-built signed zero.
#  10. sqrt/sqrtf: the subnormal normalization x * 0x1p52 (x * 0x1p23f)
#      is rebuilt with integer operations (exponent from the mantissa's
#      highest set bit); musl's exponent compensation is kept (top -= 52
#      for binary64, ix -= 23U << 23 for binary32).
#  11. the NaN result is the fixed quiet NaN for sqrt/sqrtf/fmod/fmodf;
#      fabs/fabsf/copysign/copysignf only touch the sign bit and keep the
#      payload; the rounding functions return a NaN unchanged.  The rules
#      hold for quiet and signalling NaNs alike.
#  12. the "hidden" visibility annotations are removed.
#  13. __rsqrt_tab -> __os01_libm_rsqrt_tab (the table is a non-static
#      global in the shipped archive; musl's name could collide).
#
# The fs_fabs helper of the two scanners is described above: it is an
# OS01 file that clears the sign bit with integer bit operations.
#
# Verification recorded with this vendoring (2026-11-21):
#   - all 16 symbols were differentially tested against the host glibc:
#     6.6M cases (special-value cross products, 300k random doubles, the
#     full float subnormal sweep and a sampled double subnormal sweep)
#     with zero bit-level mismatches;
#   - the errno contract table of the design spec was checked case by
#     case (24 cases, all matching);
#   - the compiled objects contain no FP arithmetic instruction and no
#     soft-float helper symbol.
#
# Shipped-state verification (2026-11-21, after the archive was wired
# into the sysroot):
#   - the host test (hosttests/cases/test_libc_math.c) compares the
#     shipped objects with a standalone glibc oracle over 272 cases
#     (136 binary64 + 136 binary32): 234 compared bit for bit, 8 as
#     NaN, 30 against the fixed quiet NaN; 258 require errno unchanged
#     and 14 require EDOM;
#   - the guest case 64_libm (user/systest/test_libm.c) runs the same
#     contract inside the running system, over 68 bit-pattern
#     assertions;
#   - the instruction audit (scripts/audit_libm_asm.py, reached through
#     `make test-libm-asm`) reads the disassembly of the PUBLISHED
#     archive on both profiles: no floating-point arithmetic, FMA,
#     rounding or conversion instruction, and the floating-point
#     registers are touched only by data moves.
